Day 24
---------

X-Path
-------
It is representing an address of an element, it is derived from XML

DOM -> Document Object Model
*Based on DOM Xpath will work

2 types of xpath
------------------
1)Absolute xpath(full xpath)          -> /html/body/div[2]/div/div/form/div[1]/div/input
It navigate from HTML to destination path

2)Relative xpath(partial xpath)       ->//*[@id="username"]
It directly jump to the element


Which xpath will be preferred?
Relative xpath.

Difference btwn Absolute & Relative xpaths?
------------------------------------------

1)Absolute xpath starts with /       ---->represents root node
  Relative xpath starts with // 

2) Absolute xpath do not use attributes
   Relative xpath works with attribute

3)Absolute xpath traverse through each node till it finds element
  Relative xpath directly jump and find element by using attribute.



RELATIVE XPATHS
-----------------
1)Automatically - Devtools,selectorshub
2)Manually(own xpath)

syntax:
-------
//tagname[@attribute='value']
//*[@attribute='value']
here tagname is not optional we replace it by *

ex: //img[@title='MacBook'] 
if we use "" we have to write like this  -> //img[@title=/"MacBook"/]

Xpath with single attribute
------
ex: driver.findElement(By.xpath("//*[@id='username']")).sendKeys("Kavana");

Xpath with multiple attribute
ex: input[@id='username'][@name='username'] -we can write it manually



xpath with 'and' 'or' operators:
----------------------------

and operator -> //input[@id='username' and @name='username'] -->both attribute should match 
or opearator -> //input[@id='username' or @name='username']  -->here anyone attribute should be match



xpath with inner text - text()
-----------------------------

//a[text()='Desktops']

A linked text can be a inner text but not all inner text be linked text 
for example:

<a href="https://xyz.com">Click me</a>

linktext=yes
innertext=yes

<div>Welcome</div>
linktext=no
innertext=yes

xpath with contains()
----------------------

driver.findElement(By.xpath("//label[contains(text(),'User')]"));


handling dynamic attributes
-----------------------------

//*[@id='start' or @id='stop']
//*[contains(@id,'st')]
//*[starts-with(@id,'st')]

Chained xpath->combination of relative and absolute xpaths
-------------
ex: div[@id='logo']/a/img



